Shoals.Dupire.du_cubic_log_moneyness_interp: grid times must be strictly increasing: index 1 has time NaN, which does not exceed time 0.25 at index 0

A NaN grid time has no position relative to any other time, so it cannot be
strictly greater than its predecessor and the same `gt` rejects it. Pinned as
its own case because that is a consequence of the comparison rather than a
separate branch: rewriting the predicate as "not less than or equal" would
preserve every other case here and silently readmit NaN.

The NaN does not reach the answer, which is what earns it a case of its own.
`linear_interp_sorted` finds no bracket at all, falls through to its
past-the-end branch and returns the last row's level: the query at t=0.375
came back 0.45, a finite confident wrong implied vol where 0.30 is correct,
with no NaN for a caller to test for.

The scope is two or more grid times. `du_first_unsorted` folds over
`range(1, len(times))`, which is empty at n=1, so a one-row surface with a NaN
time is accepted and reads flat. A leading NaN is reported at index 1, because
it is the successor comparison that fails. Admitting a lone NaN time is a
non-finite-axis question, not an ordering one, and is deliberately not fixed
here.
